  "answerContract": [
    "Countable noun cues explicitly say “the …” when the expected Italian includes an article. Calendar day/month names explicitly use bare dictionary forms.",
    "Infinitive cards use English “to …”; gender-specific people have explicit gender cues.",
    "Unspecified singular adjectives accept authored masculine/feminine alternatives. Plurals are not silently accepted when singular is requested.",
    "Common alternatives include frigo, TV, lavello, ciotola, sofà, bici, auto, hotel, calmo/calma, contento/contenta, qui/qua, lì/là and adesso/ora.",
    "Written accents remain meaningful: tè/te, lì/li and lunedì/lunedi are not collapsed. Curly and straight apostrophes, ordinary case and terminal punctuation use the existing answer normalizer.",
    "Targets are individual words or natural phrases, never slash-separated instructions requiring unrelated forms in one typed answer.",
    "Plural reminders include irregular gender changes such as l’uovo/le uova, il braccio/le braccia and il dito/le dita. Mass nouns do not receive misleading routine plurals."
  ],
